You can have a map of choices to dialogues. So for example, when you create a ChoiceDialogue, you can pass in a list of options and for each option, a corresponding Dialogue. So when the user selects an option, you can look up the corresponding Dialogue from the map and proceed.
Here's a rough sketch:
public class ChoiceDialogue extends Dialogue {
    private Map<String, Dialogue> optionsToDialogues;

    public ChoiceDialogue(String question, Map<String, Dialogue> optionsToDialogues) {
        super(question);
        this.optionsToDialogues = optionsToDialogues;
    }

    public void display() {
        // display the question
        System.out.println(getQuestion());
        // display the options
        for (String option : optionsToDialogues.keySet()) {
            System.out.println(option);
        }
        // get user input
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        String choice = scanner.nextLine();
        // get the next dialogue
        Dialogue nextDialogue = optionsToDialogues.get(choice);
        if (nextDialogue != null) {
            nextDialogue.display();
        } else {
            // handle invalid choice
        }
    }
}
Then, when you create a ChoiceDialogue, you can do something like:
Map<String, Dialogue> choices = new HashMap<>();
choices.put("Option 1", dialogue1);
choices.put("Option 2", dialogue2);
ChoiceDialogue choiceDialogue = new ChoiceDialogue("What do you want to do?", choices);
choiceDialogue.display();
